ame what do you mean by  that  will well gay shame is  um growing up like i just sort of  alluded to growing up in a society where  one doesn't feel the norm so i didn't  feel the norm i didn't feel  accepted uh i had you know if you  if you don't have acceptance at home or  you don't feel you can open up at home i  mean i was at boarding school which was  horrific in itself so i didn't spend so  much time at home  you don't have safety in the education  system and you don't have safety in the  wider society  even though when you get to a point my  mother messaging me  hang on a sec um even though you get to  a point  when um you come out you've still got  a historical amount of trauma and shame  in your body  and that's what leads to um abuse  uh you know of oneself that's what leads  to  addiction um so it's something that's  really underexplored and that's why i  wanted to write the book  and talk about it in my own life and how  i got through it  yeah one of the things that philip said  this morning in his interview was that  he sits on that sofa in the itv studio  day in day out and he talks to people  who are  confronting their own truths that he  asks those questions he hears them  you yourself have sat there on that sofa  and that he felt it was now the time to  be honest  with himself as well as with the public  how much did you feel that  responsibility as someone in the public  eye  to come out i didn't feel responsibility  because it was so much  it was it was if i felt responsibility  it was to myself  um and i remember people did suggest  that because at that time pop stars  didn't come out you know you just sort  of  you just sort of hide it you know and i  just thought the idea of  pretending to you know walk around with  sort of staged photographs of me with a  pretend girlfriend  would just crush me because i'd already  come out did you come under pressure to  do that well  yeah oh gosh yes i mean i had you know i  say this in the book you know  i 